00:00:35.322 --> 00:00:37.242
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:38.492 --> 00:00:41.393
A story: the sale that autoscaling couldn't save.

13
00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:47.241
A grocery platform announced a Diwali sale starting at 8:00 pm sharp:

14
00:00:47.521 --> 00:00:50.743
1 rupeesonions, 60 % off dry fruits.

15
00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:52.580
Engineering was confident.

16
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:59.665
Every service had a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) at 60 % CPU, and the cloud scales.

17
00:00:59.945 --> 00:01:10.164
At 8:00:00 pm, gateway traffic went from 9 K req per second to 170 K req per second in 40 seconds.

18
00:01:10.444 --> 00:01:12.279
8:00:15.

19
00:01:12.559 --> 00:01:15.396
Prometheus scraped the first high CPU samples.

20
00:01:15.676 --> 00:01:17.319
8:00:30.

21
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:22.313
The HPA decided to scale catalog-service from 12 to 60 pods.

22
00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:25.687
8:00:30 to 8:02:30.

23
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:27.801
The cluster had no spare nodes.

24
00:01:28.081 --> 00:01:32.881
The node autoscaler requested 30 machines, which took about 3 minutes to join.

25
00:01:34.231 --> 00:01:35.661
8:03:40.

26
00:01:35.941 --> 00:01:38.394
New JVMs finished starting and warming up.

27
00:01:38.674 --> 00:01:41.895
For roughly four minutes, the existing pods were overloaded.

28
00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:48.085
Latency rose, clients timed out and retried, which multiplied load by 2 to 3 times.

29
00:01:48.365 --> 00:01:54.082
Liveness checks failed, Kubernetes restarted unhealthy pods, and capacity dropped further.

30
00:01:54.362 --> 00:02:00.826
The first 10 minutes of the sale, the most valuable 10 minutes of the quarter, returned errors to most customers.

31
00:02:02.176 --> 00:02:10.240
Nobody had done the arithmetic: peak ÷ per-pod capacity = pods needed before 8:00 pm.

32
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.643
A ten-minute estimate on a whiteboard would have shown that reactive autoscaling could not react in time.

33
00:02:16.923 --> 00:02:20.251
The fix was trivial: pre-scale at 7:30 pm.

34
00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:22.283
Quote:

35
00:02:22.563 --> 00:02:24.227
The thesis of this chapter.

36
00:02:24.507 --> 00:02:27.131
Estimation isn't about getting the right number.

37
00:02:27.411 --> 00:02:32.638
It's about finding which part of the design the numbers break, and doing that before production does.

38
