319
00:14:33.193 --> 00:14:34.579
Storage estimation.

320
00:14:34.859 --> 00:14:35.841
The formula:

321
00:14:36.121 --> 00:14:42.179
Storage = records per day times bytes or record times retention days times overhead.

322
00:14:43.529 --> 00:14:52.703
The overhead multiplier covers indexes ( times 1.5 to 3), replication ( times 2 to 3) and headroom ( times 1.3).

323
00:14:52.983 --> 00:14:54.625
Worked example: orders.

324
00:14:55.975 --> 00:14:57.341
Here is a quick table:

325
00:14:57.621 --> 00:14:58.943
Item, and Estimate.

326
00:14:59.223 --> 00:15:04.749
Order header row: ~1 KB (IDs, amounts, address snapshot, status, timestamps).

327
00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:06.095
Line items:

328
00:15:06.375 --> 00:15:10.407
8 items times ~250 B = 2 KB.

329
00:15:10.687 --> 00:15:11.839
Status history:

330
00:15:12.119 --> 00:15:16.386
6 transitions times ~150 B about 1 KB.

331
00:15:16.666 --> 00:15:18.970
Raw per order: ~4 KB.

332
00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:24.242
Orders per day (year-round average incl. festivals): ~500 K.

333
00:15:24.522 --> 00:15:25.461
Raw per day:

334
00:15:25.741 --> 00:15:29.538
500 K times 4 KB = 2 GB per day.

335
00:15:29.818 --> 00:15:32.698
Per year: ~730 GB raw.

336
00:15:34.048 --> 00:15:42.539
times indexes (2) times copies (3 = primary + 2 replicas): ~4.4 TB per year provisioned.

337
00:15:42.819 --> 00:15:46.851
GST record retention (multi-year, confirm with finance):

338
00:15:47.131 --> 00:15:50.480
Hot data 1 year in OLTP, older in a cheap archive.

339
00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:52.747
Success:

340
00:15:53.027 --> 00:15:54.499
What the estimate tells us.

341
00:15:54.779 --> 00:16:00.113
A few TB per year in PostgreSQL is fine for one well-provisioned cluster.

342
00:16:00.393 --> 00:16:02.825
There's no need to shard orders on day one.

343
00:16:03.105 --> 00:16:09.675
But plan partitioning by month so old data moves to archive cheaply (Ch 05, 28).

344
00:16:09.955 --> 00:16:13.817
The estimate turned should we shard? into not yet, and here's the trigger.

345
00:16:15.167 --> 00:16:17.151
Other storage in the platform (rough):

346
00:16:18.501 --> 00:16:19.866
Here is a quick table:

347
00:16:20.146 --> 00:16:22.023
Data, and Volume, and Store.

348
00:16:22.303 --> 00:16:24.074
GST invoice PDFs.

349
00:16:24.354 --> 00:16:25.186
Volume:

350
00:16:25.466 --> 00:16:32.442
500 K per day times 60 KB = 30 GB per day, then ~11 TB per year.

351
00:16:32.722 --> 00:16:33.511
Store:

352
00:16:33.791 --> 00:16:36.885
Object storage + lifecycle to a cold tier.

353
00:16:37.165 --> 00:16:38.658
Rider location pings.

354
00:16:38.938 --> 00:16:39.770
Volume:

355
00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:48.711
40 K riders times 1 ping or 4 s = 10 K writes per second times 100 B about 86 GB per day.

356
00:16:48.991 --> 00:16:49.781
Store:

357
00:16:50.061 --> 00:16:53.453
Redis (latest) + time-series (7-day retention).

358
00:16:53.733 --> 00:16:54.906
Product images.

359
00:16:55.186 --> 00:16:56.018
Volume:

360
00:16:56.298 --> 00:17:04.511
250 K SKUs times 6 images times 4 sizes times 60 KB about 360 GB.

361
00:17:04.791 --> 00:17:05.581
Store:

362
00:17:05.861 --> 00:17:07.759
Object storage + CDN.

363
00:17:08.039 --> 00:17:09.383
Application logs.

364
00:17:09.663 --> 00:17:10.495
Volume:

365
00:17:10.775 --> 00:17:18.690
26 services times ~3 KB per request times 80 M requests about 240 GB per day.

366
00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:19.759
Store:

367
00:17:20.039 --> 00:17:23.346
Often the largest cost line; sample and set retention.

368
00:17:24.696 --> 00:17:26.104
Bandwidth estimation.

369
00:17:26.384 --> 00:17:27.365
The formula:

370
00:17:27.645 --> 00:17:30.461
Bandwidth = QPS times response size.

371
00:17:31.811 --> 00:17:33.945
Sale start, catalog API:

372
00:17:34.225 --> 00:17:43.825
110 K req per second times 20 KB JSON about 2.2 GB per second about 18 Gbps uncompressed.

373
00:17:44.105 --> 00:17:51.251
With gzip or Brotli (~5 to 8 times on JSON), that's ~2 to 3.5 Gbps.

374
00:17:51.531 --> 00:17:56.545
Images go to the CDN, so they aren't your origin's problem, but they are your bill:

375
00:17:56.825 --> 00:18:04.633
6 M DAU times 40 images times 40 KB about 9.6 TB per day of CDN egress.

376
00:18:05.983 --> 00:18:06.623
Tip:

377
00:18:06.903 --> 00:18:09.591
Bandwidth estimates catch a class of bugs early.

378
00:18:09.871 --> 00:18:21.049
A product-list API returning full product objects (80 KB) instead of list DTOs (8 KB) is a 10 times bandwidth and serialization-CPU bug.

379
00:18:21.329 --> 00:18:26.769
Estimating response sizes during API design (Ch 04) prevents it.

380
00:18:28.119 --> 00:18:29.485
Memory estimation.

381
00:18:29.765 --> 00:18:31.066
Can it fit in memory?

382
00:18:31.346 --> 00:18:35.975
That question decides between an in-process cache, Redis, and must go to disk.

383
00:18:37.325 --> 00:18:38.307
The formula:

384
00:18:38.587 --> 00:18:42.939
Memory = entries times (key + value + per-entry overhead).

385
00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:45.654
Here is a quick table:

386
00:18:45.934 --> 00:18:48.153
Structure, and Rough per-entry overhead.

387
00:18:48.433 --> 00:18:55.217
Java HashMap entry (node + refs): ~32 to 48 B + key and value objects.

388
00:18:55.497 --> 00:19:01.150
Java String (short, compact strings): ~40 B + 1 B or char (Latin-1).

389
00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:04.950
Redis small key-value: ~50 to 90 B overhead.

390
00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:07.964
Example: carts in Redis.

391
00:19:08.244 --> 00:19:20.105
1.5 M active carts at peak times (8 items times 60 B + 200 B overhead) about 1.5 M times 680 B about 1 GB.

392
00:19:20.385 --> 00:19:26.188
That fits comfortably in a single Redis shard (with a replica), so memory doesn't force clustering.

393
00:19:26.468 --> 00:19:29.647
The deciding factor is ops per second (§4).

394
00:19:30.997 --> 00:19:32.789
Server count and Little's Law.

395
00:19:33.069 --> 00:19:34.541
CPU-based sizing.

396
00:19:34.821 --> 00:19:43.930
The formula: cores needed = (peak QPS times CPU ms per request) / (1000 times target utilization).

397
00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:58.336
Checkout at 850 req per second times 6 ms CPU per request ÷ (1000 times 0.5) about 10.2 cores, then 6 pods times 2 vCPU.

398
00:19:59.686 --> 00:20:00.433
Note:

399
00:20:00.713 --> 00:20:04.851
Why target 50 to 60 % utilization, not 90 %?.

400
00:20:05.131 --> 00:20:11.254
Queueing theory (M or M or 1 intuition): waiting time grows as ρ / (1 minus ρ).

401
00:20:11.534 --> 00:20:15.310
At 50 % utilization the multiplier is 1 times.

402
00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:17.297
At 80 % it's 4 times.

403
00:20:17.577 --> 00:20:20.073
At 95 % it's 19 times.

404
00:20:20.353 --> 00:20:25.942
Tail latency explodes long before CPU hits 100 % (Ch 25).

405
00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:28.999
Little's Law: in-flight work.

406
00:20:29.279 --> 00:20:30.260
The formula:

407
00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:35.916
L = lambda times W (in-flight = arrival rate times time in system).

408
00:20:37.266 --> 00:20:38.631
Here is a quick table:

409
00:20:38.911 --> 00:20:41.066
Use, and Calculation, and Result.

410
00:20:41.346 --> 00:20:42.967
Concurrent checkout requests.

411
00:20:43.247 --> 00:20:44.314
Calculation:

412
00:20:44.594 --> 00:20:48.754
850 req per second times 0.15 s.

413
00:20:49.034 --> 00:20:51.594
Result: ~128 in flight.

414
00:20:51.874 --> 00:20:53.858
DB connections checkout needs.

415
00:20:54.138 --> 00:20:55.205
Calculation:

416
00:20:55.485 --> 00:21:00.583
850 req per second times 20 ms of DB time per request.

417
00:21:00.863 --> 00:21:03.338
Result: ~17 connections busy.

418
00:21:03.618 --> 00:21:05.154
Threads (platform threads).

419
00:21:05.434 --> 00:21:06.501
Calculation:

420
00:21:06.781 --> 00:21:09.447
128 in flight + headroom.

421
00:21:09.727 --> 00:21:13.503
Result: ~200 (virtual threads remove this limit).

422
00:21:13.783 --> 00:21:15.554
Kafka consumer parallelism.

423
00:21:15.834 --> 00:21:16.901
Calculation:

424
00:21:17.181 --> 00:21:20.786
1,000 events per second times 30 ms processing.

425
00:21:21.066 --> 00:21:24.693
Result: ≥ 30 partitions or consumers.

426
00:21:26.043 --> 00:21:26.960
Watch out:

427
00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.499
The connection budget.

428
00:21:28.779 --> 00:21:35.072
PostgreSQL connections are expensive (several MB each, with contention above a few hundred).

429
00:21:35.352 --> 00:21:44.739
If 26 services times 6 pods times a pool of 20 all point at shared clusters, that's 3,120 potential connections.

430
00:21:45.019 --> 00:21:48.069
Little's Law says you need far fewer busy connections.

431
00:21:48.349 --> 00:21:55.795
Size pools from Little's Law, not from defaults, and add PgBouncer as pod counts grow (Ch 05).

432
00:21:57.145 --> 00:22:00.259
Worked example: the festival-sale checkout estimate.

433
00:22:00.539 --> 00:22:03.675
Given: a festival day with 1.5 M orders.

434
00:22:03.955 --> 00:22:06.857
The sale starts at 8:00 pm.

435
00:22:07.137 --> 00:22:13.537
20 % of the day's orders happen in the first hour, and minute one runs at 5 times that hour's average.

436
00:22:14.887 --> 00:22:16.252
Here is a quick table:

437
00:22:16.532 --> 00:22:18.665
Step, and Calculation, and Result.

438
00:22:18.945 --> 00:22:20.417
Orders in the first hour.

439
00:22:20.697 --> 00:22:21.764
Calculation:

440
00:22:22.044 --> 00:22:24.497
1.5 M times 0.2.

441
00:22:24.777 --> 00:22:25.588
Result:

442
00:22:25.868 --> 00:22:27.063
300 K.

443
00:22:27.343 --> 00:22:29.561
Average orders per second in that hour.

444
00:22:29.841 --> 00:22:30.908
Calculation:

445
00:22:31.188 --> 00:22:34.623
300 K ÷ 3,600.

446
00:22:34.903 --> 00:22:37.036
Result: ~83 per second.

447
00:22:37.316 --> 00:22:39.001
Peak-minute orders per second.

448
00:22:39.281 --> 00:22:40.348
Calculation:

449
00:22:40.628 --> 00:22:42.036
83 times 5.

450
00:22:42.316 --> 00:22:45.260
Result: ~420 orders per second.

451
00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:49.508
Checkout attempts (payment failures, retries, drop-offs: times 2).

452
00:22:49.788 --> 00:22:50.855
Calculation:

453
00:22:51.135 --> 00:22:52.863
420 times 2.

454
00:22:53.143 --> 00:22:56.428
Result: ~850 checkout req per second.

455
00:22:56.708 --> 00:22:59.652
Concurrent users at 8:00 pm.

456
00:22:59.932 --> 00:23:00.999
Calculation:

457
00:23:01.279 --> 00:23:04.543
6 M DAU times ~13 % online.

458
00:23:04.823 --> 00:23:06.785
Result: ~800 K.

459
00:23:07.065 --> 00:23:10.244
Gateway requests (1 request per 4 s per user).

460
00:23:10.524 --> 00:23:11.591
Calculation:

461
00:23:11.871 --> 00:23:14.025
800 K ÷ 4.

462
00:23:14.305 --> 00:23:20.108
Result: ~200 K req per second. ...of which catalog or search or product reads.

463
00:23:20.388 --> 00:23:22.969
Calculation: ~55 %.

464
00:23:23.249 --> 00:23:26.236
Result: ~110 K req per second.

465
00:23:27.586 --> 00:23:34.967
Reservations for the 1 rupeesonion SKU (in 30 % of orders, spread over ~50 busy stores).

466
00:23:35.247 --> 00:23:36.314
Calculation:

467
00:23:36.594 --> 00:23:40.413
850 times 0.3 ÷ 50.

468
00:23:40.693 --> 00:23:47.519
Result: ~5 per second per store-SKU row. ...if one metro store gets 20 % of that demand.

469
00:23:47.799 --> 00:23:48.866
Calculation:

470
00:23:49.146 --> 00:23:52.751
850 times 0.3 times 0.2.

471
00:23:53.031 --> 00:23:59.559
Result: ~50 per second on one row: fine for a row lock (~500 per second max).

472
00:23:59.839 --> 00:24:03.402
Order DB statements (order + items + outbox).

473
00:24:03.682 --> 00:24:04.749
Calculation:

474
00:24:05.029 --> 00:24:07.375
420 times ~12.

475
00:24:07.655 --> 00:24:11.581
Result: ~5 K statements per second on the orders primary.

476
00:24:11.861 --> 00:24:13.397
Payment webhook callbacks.

477
00:24:13.677 --> 00:24:17.623
Calculation: ~420 per second + retries.

478
00:24:17.903 --> 00:24:18.714
Result:

479
00:24:18.994 --> 00:24:22.407
Idempotent handler required (Ch 20, 45).

480
00:24:22.687 --> 00:24:24.863
Kafka order.order.placed.v1.

481
00:24:25.143 --> 00:24:26.210
Calculation:

482
00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.093
420 per second times 2 KB.

483
00:24:29.373 --> 00:24:36.391
Result: ~0.8 MB per second: tiny for Kafka; partitions sized by consumer time.

484
00:24:37.741 --> 00:24:38.659
Success:

485
00:24:38.939 --> 00:24:41.541
What this estimate tells us (the real value).

486
00:24:41.821 --> 00:24:47.987
Checkout compute is not the problem. ~850 req per second needs ~6 pods.

487
00:24:48.267 --> 00:24:59.915
Read traffic is the problem. ~110 K req per second of catalog needs CDN plus multi-layer caching, and pre-scaling (Ch 07, 21).

488
00:25:00.195 --> 00:25:02.883
Contention is a watch item, not a crisis.

489
00:25:03.163 --> 00:25:07.237
The hot SKU row sits at ~10 % of lock capacity.

490
00:25:07.517 --> 00:25:12.637
We'd re-estimate if the sale became a single national flash deal (Ch 46).

491
00:25:12.917 --> 00:25:14.219
Rise time kills.

492
00:25:14.499 --> 00:25:21.539
From 9 K to 200 K req per second in about 40 s is faster than any reactive autoscaler.

493
00:25:21.819 --> 00:25:22.715
Pre-scale.

494
00:25:24.065 --> 00:25:26.689
Estimation in interviews versus at work.

495
00:25:26.969 --> 00:25:28.334
