00:48:41.399 --> 00:48:42.807
ADR-014:

918
00:48:43.087 --> 00:48:44.879
Pre-scale for scheduled events.

919
00:48:45.159 --> 00:48:46.077
Context.

920
00:48:46.357 --> 00:48:50.666
Scheduled sale starts rise 15 to 40 times within about a minute.

921
00:48:50.946 --> 00:48:58.754
Reactive scaling needs 2 to 5 minutes (scrape + HPA sync + node provisioning + JVM warm-up).

922
00:48:59.034 --> 00:49:03.578
The incident in §1.1 cost ~4 minutes of errors at peak.

923
00:49:03.858 --> 00:49:04.733
Decision.

924
00:49:05.013 --> 00:49:07.807
For events forecast above 3 times normal peak:

925
00:49:08.087 --> 00:49:28.823
(1) generate the plan from the last comparable event's measured peak times growth times P90; (2) size with measured per-pod capacity and one-zone-loss headroom; (3) apply a KEDA cron floor from Tminus 30 min to event end; (4) keep a Prometheus trigger for reactive scaling above the floor; (5) block

926
00:49:29.103 --> 00:49:36.165
manifest generation while the plan has blockers; (6) gateway load shedding is mandatory as the backstop.

927
00:49:37.515 --> 00:49:38.539
Consequences.

928
00:49:38.819 --> 00:49:41.485
(+) Capacity is in place before the spike.

929
00:49:41.765 --> 00:49:43.984
(+) The plan is reviewable in Git.

930
00:49:44.264 --> 00:49:54.845
(minus ) We pay for idle capacity for ~30 minutes before the event and during low-demand parts of the window (~11 lakh rupees per major event).

931
00:49:55.125 --> 00:50:00.608
(minus ) The forecast can still be wrong, which is why the reactive trigger and load shedding remain.

932
00:50:00.888 --> 00:50:15.160
Revisit when: start-time staggering is accepted by marketing, or warm-up drops below ~30 s (for example with CRaC or AOT-cached JVMs), making reactive scaling viable for part of the spike.

