00:53:59.853 --> 00:54:04.803
Retry budgets, jittered backoff, Retry-After honored by clients (Ch 18).

1039
00:54:05.083 --> 00:54:07.067
Liveness probe tied to latency.

1040
00:54:07.347 --> 00:54:08.541
Detection signal:

1041
00:54:08.821 --> 00:54:10.677
Restart storms during overload.

1042
00:54:10.957 --> 00:54:12.173
Blast radius:

1043
00:54:12.453 --> 00:54:13.819
Capacity collapses.

1044
00:54:14.099 --> 00:54:15.101
Mitigation:

1045
00:54:15.381 --> 00:54:18.987
Liveness = process health only; readiness for traffic.

1046
00:54:20.337 --> 00:54:22.150
Observability for capacity.

1047
00:54:22.430 --> 00:54:23.795
Here is a quick table:

1048
00:54:24.075 --> 00:54:29.110
Signal, and Type, and Why. httpserverrequestssecondscount rate per service.

1049
00:54:29.390 --> 00:54:30.051
Type:

1050
00:54:30.331 --> 00:54:31.611
Counter, then rate.

1051
00:54:31.891 --> 00:54:32.574
Why:

1052
00:54:32.854 --> 00:54:34.518
Demand input for L4.

1053
00:54:34.798 --> 00:54:36.526
Per-pod throughput at SLO.

1054
00:54:36.806 --> 00:54:37.467
Type:

1055
00:54:37.747 --> 00:54:42.483
Derived (rate ÷ ready pods, when p99 within SLO).

1056
00:54:42.763 --> 00:54:43.446
Why:

1057
00:54:43.726 --> 00:54:46.329
Validates load-test capacity in production.

1058
00:54:46.609 --> 00:54:52.667
Headroom % = (pods times per-pod capacity minus load) ÷ capacity.

1059
00:54:52.947 --> 00:54:53.609
Type:

1060
00:54:53.889 --> 00:54:54.913
Recording rule.

1061
00:54:55.193 --> 00:54:55.875
Why:

1062
00:54:56.155 --> 00:54:58.225
The single most useful event-day number.

1063
00:54:58.505 --> 00:55:01.257
Hikari hikaricpconnectionsactive or pending.

1064
00:55:01.537 --> 00:55:02.198
Type:

1065
00:55:02.478 --> 00:55:03.289
Gauge.

1066
00:55:03.569 --> 00:55:04.251
Why:

1067
00:55:04.531 --> 00:55:08.329
Little's Law in practice; pending 0 means pool saturation.

1068
00:55:08.609 --> 00:55:09.953
Kafka consumer lag.

1069
00:55:10.233 --> 00:55:10.894
Type:

1070
00:55:11.174 --> 00:55:11.985
Gauge.

1071
00:55:12.265 --> 00:55:12.947
Why:

1072
00:55:13.227 --> 00:55:14.785
Consumers under-provisioned.

1073
00:55:16.135 --> 00:55:18.396
Pending pods, node provisioning time.

1074
00:55:18.676 --> 00:55:19.337
Type:

1075
00:55:19.617 --> 00:55:20.428
Gauge.

1076
00:55:20.708 --> 00:55:21.391
Why:

1077
00:55:21.671 --> 00:55:23.100
Scaling mechanics health.

1078
00:55:23.380 --> 00:55:24.980
Actual versus forecast.

1079
00:55:25.260 --> 00:55:25.921
Type:

1080
00:55:26.201 --> 00:55:27.311
Dashboard panel.

1081
00:55:27.591 --> 00:55:28.273
Why:

1082
00:55:28.553 --> 00:55:30.388
Calibrates next year's estimate.

1083
00:55:31.738 --> 00:55:37.647
Example Prometheus recording rule for headroom (catalog, 3,000 req per second per pod):

1084
00:55:38.997 --> 00:55:40.469
Here is the YAML code.

1085
00:55:40.749 --> 00:55:44.248
It runs to 16 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

1086
00:55:47.098 --> 00:55:48.037
Security.

1087
00:55:48.317 --> 00:55:52.583
Capacity plans reveal business forecasts (order volumes, growth).

1088
00:55:52.863 --> 00:55:57.130
Treat planner output as confidential and keep it in a restricted repo.

1089
00:55:57.410 --> 00:56:01.570
The planner's Prometheus access should be read-only, through a service account.

1090
00:56:01.850 --> 00:56:06.309
Load tests must not hit real payment gateways or send real SMS.

1091
00:56:06.589 --> 00:56:10.791
Use sandbox endpoints (Razorpay test mode) and stub providers.

1092
00:56:12.141 --> 00:56:13.805
Performance and tuning knobs.

1093
00:56:14.085 --> 00:56:15.451
Here is a quick table:

1094
00:56:15.731 --> 00:56:18.056
Knob, and Typical value, and Guidance.

1095
00:56:18.336 --> 00:56:19.723
Target utilization.

1096
00:56:20.003 --> 00:56:21.091
Typical value:

1097
00:56:21.371 --> 00:56:23.461
50 to 60 % CPU.

1098
00:56:23.741 --> 00:56:24.637
Guidance:

1099
00:56:24.917 --> 00:56:30.251
Lower for latency-critical paths; queueing grows as ρ/(1minus ρ).

1100
00:56:30.531 --> 00:56:31.768
P90 factor.

1101
00:56:32.048 --> 00:56:33.136
Typical value:

1102
00:56:33.416 --> 00:56:35.677
1.15 to 1.5.

1103
00:56:35.957 --> 00:56:36.853
Guidance:

1104
00:56:37.133 --> 00:56:39.203
Derive from forecast error history.

1105
00:56:39.483 --> 00:56:40.805
Pre-scale lead time.

1106
00:56:41.085 --> 00:56:42.173
Typical value:

1107
00:56:42.453 --> 00:56:43.392
30 min.

1108
00:56:43.672 --> 00:56:48.749
Guidance: ≥ node provisioning + JVM warm-up + safety.

1109
00:56:49.029 --> 00:56:50.032
Zone headroom.

1110
00:56:50.312 --> 00:56:53.768
Typical value: zones ÷ (zones minus 1).

1111
00:56:54.048 --> 00:56:54.944
Guidance:

1112
00:56:55.224 --> 00:57:00.109
1.5 for 3 AZs; 2.0 for 2 AZs (expensive).

1113
00:57:00.389 --> 00:57:01.797
Hikari pool size.

1114
00:57:02.077 --> 00:57:03.165
Typical value:

1115
00:57:03.445 --> 00:57:05.685
Little's Law times 1.5 to 2.

1116
00:57:05.965 --> 00:57:06.861
Guidance:

1117
00:57:07.141 --> 00:57:09.616
Not the default of 10 for every service.

1118
00:57:09.896 --> 00:57:11.581
KEDA Prometheus threshold.

1119
00:57:11.861 --> 00:57:15.829
Typical value: per-pod capacity times target utilization.

1120
00:57:16.109 --> 00:57:17.005
Guidance:

1121
00:57:17.285 --> 00:57:20.165
So reactive scaling starts before saturation.

1122
00:57:21.515 --> 00:57:22.241
Cost.

1123
00:57:22.521 --> 00:57:24.483
A capacity plan is a cost plan.

1124
00:57:24.763 --> 00:57:26.235
For the Diwali example:

1125
00:57:27.585 --> 00:57:28.951
Here is a quick table:

1126
00:57:29.231 --> 00:57:30.553
Item, and Estimate.

1127
00:57:30.833 --> 00:57:40.391
Extra pods (~200 times 1.5 vCPU times 3.5 h): ~1,050 vCPU-hours.

1128
00:57:40.671 --> 00:57:43.657
Reserved node capacity (a day's reservation):

1129
00:57:43.937 --> 00:57:45.324
Largest line item.

1130
00:57:45.604 --> 00:57:49.231
CDN egress (~9.6 TB extra):

1131
00:57:49.511 --> 00:57:52.220
Per-GB pricing, negotiate committed-use.

1132
00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:58.132
Load-test environment (D-5, 4 h at stress): ~equal to one event hour.

1133
00:57:59.482 --> 00:58:10.085
Show finance the cost of the alternative: last year's four minutes of errors at ~420 orders per second is ~100 K lost or delayed orders.

1134
00:58:10.365 --> 00:58:19.837
At an average basket of 700, rupeesthat's ~7 crore rupees of GMV at risk against ~11 lakh rupees of capacity.

1135
00:58:21.187 --> 00:58:22.125
Part 7.

1136
00:58:22.405 --> 00:58:24.112
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1137
00:58:25.362 --> 00:58:26.002
1.

1138
00:58:26.282 --> 00:58:27.455
Precision theatre.

1139
00:58:27.735 --> 00:58:28.546
Symptom:

1140
00:58:28.826 --> 00:58:34.309
83.333 orders per second in an interview, while the peak factor is a guess.

1141
00:58:34.589 --> 00:58:35.314
Fix:

1142
00:58:35.594 --> 00:58:40.927
Round aggressively (10⁵ seconds per day) and spend the time on what the numbers imply.

1143
00:58:41.207 --> 00:58:41.911
2.

1144
00:58:42.191 --> 00:58:43.621
Sizing on averages.

1145
00:58:43.901 --> 00:58:44.711
Symptom:

1146
00:58:44.991 --> 00:58:52.650
We do 1 K req per second, 10 pods is plenty, then the evening peak is 5 times and a sale start is 30 times.

1147
00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:53.655
Fix:

1148
00:58:53.935 --> 00:58:58.821
Always carry a peak factor, justified by traffic shape (§2.3).

1149
00:58:59.101 --> 00:58:59.847
3.

1150
00:59:00.127 --> 00:59:03.050
Trusting reactive autoscaling for step changes.

1151
00:59:04.400 --> 00:59:16.240
Here is the YAML code. before: only reactive; nothing is ready at 20:00:00. after: cron floor for the event window + reactive above it (L4).

1152
00:59:19.090 --> 00:59:19.794
4.

1153
00:59:20.074 --> 00:59:21.781
Guessing per-pod capacity.

1154
00:59:22.061 --> 00:59:22.871
Symptom:

1155
00:59:23.151 --> 00:59:26.565
A pod can do 2,000 req per second from a blog post.

1156
00:59:26.845 --> 00:59:31.367
The real endpoint does 3 DB calls and manages 180.

1157
00:59:31.647 --> 00:59:32.373
Fix:

1158
00:59:32.653 --> 00:59:38.882
Load test each critical service to the point where p99 breaks its SLO, and use that number.

1159
00:59:39.162 --> 00:59:39.930
5.

1160
00:59:40.210 --> 00:59:42.087
Default pool sizes everywhere.

1161
00:59:43.437 --> 00:59:58.520
Here is the YAML code. before: every service, every pod, adds up to thousands of connections. after: sized from Little's Law (850 req per second times 20 ms about 17 busy across the fleet), per pod.

1162
01:00:01.370 --> 01:00:02.117
6.

1163
01:00:02.397 --> 01:00:03.805
Ignoring zone failure.

1164
01:00:04.085 --> 01:00:04.895
Symptom:

1165
01:00:05.175 --> 01:00:07.245
Capacity is exactly enough at peak.

1166
01:00:07.525 --> 01:00:10.618
One AZ outage at peak becomes a total outage.

1167
01:00:10.898 --> 01:00:18.727
Fix: zones ÷ (zones minus 1) headroom for tier-1 services, and write down which services are tier-1.

1168
01:00:19.007 --> 01:00:19.754
7.

1169
01:00:20.034 --> 01:00:21.442
Forgetting the data tier.

1170
01:00:21.722 --> 01:00:22.533
Symptom:

1171
01:00:22.813 --> 01:00:29.511
The app tier scales to 96 pods, and the database falls over from connections, CPU or hot rows.

1172
01:00:29.791 --> 01:00:30.517
Fix:

1173
01:00:30.797 --> 01:00:34.679
Every capacity plan has a data-tier check section (§4).

1174
01:00:34.959 --> 01:00:35.642
8.

1175
01:00:35.922 --> 01:00:37.181
Estimating once.

1176
01:00:37.461 --> 01:00:38.271
Symptom:

1177
01:00:38.551 --> 01:00:41.687
The plan from two years ago still drives min replicas.

1178
01:00:41.967 --> 01:00:42.693
Fix:

1179
01:00:42.973 --> 01:00:49.586
Compare actual versus forecast after every event, and recalibrate the peak factors and per-pod capacity.

1180
01:00:50.936 --> 01:00:51.811
Part 8.

1181
01:00:52.091 --> 01:00:53.435
Interview Q and A.

1182
01:00:54.685 --> 01:00:55.517
Beginner.

1183
01:00:55.797 --> 01:00:56.821
Question 1.

1184
01:00:57.101 --> 01:01:01.346
Why do back-of-the-envelope estimates in a system design interview at all?

1185
01:01:01.626 --> 01:01:02.991
Thirty-second answer:

1186
01:01:03.271 --> 01:01:05.682
To find out which parts of the design matter.

1187
01:01:05.962 --> 01:01:13.855
Estimates tell you whether data fits in memory, whether you need caching, sharding or async processing, and where the bottleneck will be.

1188
01:01:14.135 --> 01:01:16.909
Without numbers, every design choice is a guess.

1189
01:01:18.259 --> 01:01:19.261
Question 2.

1190
01:01:19.541 --> 01:01:24.021
Convert 10 million requests per day into requests per second, and give the peak.

1191
01:01:24.301 --> 01:01:25.667
Thirty-second answer:

1192
01:01:25.947 --> 01:01:32.325
10 M ÷ 10⁵ about 100 req per second average (116 exactly).

1193
01:01:32.605 --> 01:01:34.205
Peak depends on traffic shape.

1194
01:01:34.485 --> 01:01:39.968
With a typical 3 to 5 times daily peak, that's 300 to 500 req per second.

1195
01:01:40.248 --> 01:01:43.107
A scheduled event can reach 20 to 40 times.

1196
01:01:44.457 --> 01:01:45.502
Question 3.

1197
01:01:45.782 --> 01:01:49.558
Which latency numbers should every engineer know, and why do they matter?

1198
01:01:49.838 --> 01:01:51.203
Thirty-second answer:

1199
01:01:51.483 --> 01:02:09.083
Memory ~100 ns, SSD random read ~20 to 100 µs, same-AZ round trip ~0.1 to 0.5 ms, cross-region ~50 to 200 ms, and new pod or JVM start in tens of seconds.

1200
01:02:09.363 --> 01:02:15.635
They tell you what a design can achieve: memory is ~1,000 times faster than a network call.

1201
01:02:16.985 --> 01:02:18.052
Question 4.

1202
01:02:18.332 --> 01:02:21.980
Estimate storage for 1 million orders per day for one year.

1203
01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:23.625
Thirty-second answer:

1204
01:02:23.905 --> 01:02:34.401
Around 4 KB per order (header, ~8 items, status history) times 1 M per day about 4 GB per day about 1.5 TB per year raw.

1205
01:02:34.681 --> 01:02:39.396
With indexes ( times 2) and 3 copies, ~9 TB provisioned.

1206
01:02:39.676 --> 01:02:44.263
That fits a single well-provisioned relational cluster with monthly partitions.

1207
01:02:45.613 --> 01:02:46.615
Intermediate.

1208
01:02:46.895 --> 01:02:48.005
Question 5.

1209
01:02:48.285 --> 01:02:51.421
How do you derive a peak factor instead of just picking one?

1210
01:02:51.701 --> 01:02:53.066
Thirty-second answer:

1211
01:02:53.346 --> 01:03:01.303
From traffic shape: hour-of-day and day-of-week histograms, past campaigns and sale starts, and retry behaviour during incidents.

1212
01:03:01.583 --> 01:03:07.706
Take the per-second or 10-second maximum over the relevant window, divided by the daily average.

1213
01:03:09.056 --> 01:03:10.165
Question 6.

1214
01:03:10.445 --> 01:03:13.603
Use Little's Law to size a database connection pool.

1215
01:03:13.883 --> 01:03:15.248
Thirty-second answer:

1216
01:03:15.528 --> 01:03:20.712
Busy connections = request rate times time each request holds a connection.

1217
01:03:20.992 --> 01:03:27.712
At 850 req per second times 20 ms = 17 busy connections across the fleet.

1218
01:03:27.992 --> 01:03:36.547
Give 1.5 to 2 times headroom and split across pods: with 10 pods, ~4 per pod minimum, so 10 per pod is generous.

1219
01:03:37.897 --> 01:03:38.985
Question 7.

1220
01:03:39.265 --> 01:03:42.315
Why does the read or write ratio matter for design?

1221
01:03:42.595 --> 01:03:43.961
Thirty-second answer:

1222
01:03:44.241 --> 01:03:45.862
It tells you where to optimize.

1223
01:03:46.142 --> 01:03:50.707
Read-heavy workloads (100:1) want caches, replicas and CDNs.

1224
01:03:50.987 --> 01:04:00.417
Write-heavy workloads want append-friendly stores, batching and partitioning. one-on-one contended workloads need attention to locking and hot keys.

1225
01:04:01.767 --> 01:04:02.791
Question 8.

1226
01:04:03.071 --> 01:04:07.700
Estimate bandwidth for the product listing API at peak and how to reduce it.

1227
01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:09.345
Thirty-second answer:

1228
01:04:09.625 --> 01:04:18.564
110 K req per second times 20 KB about 2.2 GB per second (~18 Gbps) uncompressed.

1229
01:04:18.844 --> 01:04:34.140
Reduce it with compression (5 to 8 times on JSON), list DTOs instead of full objects, pagination limits, sparse fields, HTTP caching or CDN for anonymous listings, and images served only via CDN.

1230
01:04:35.490 --> 01:04:36.557
Question 9.

1231
01:04:36.837 --> 01:04:40.037
How many servers do you need for 20 K req per second?

1232
01:04:40.317 --> 01:04:41.682
Thirty-second answer:

1233
01:04:41.962 --> 01:04:43.647
It depends on per-request cost.

1234
01:04:43.927 --> 01:04:51.927
If a pod sustains 1,000 req per second at SLO (from a load test), that's 20 pods at 100 % utilization.

1235
01:04:52.207 --> 01:04:54.853
At a 60 % target, ~34.

1236
01:04:55.133 --> 01:04:59.421
With 3-AZ headroom, ~51, rounded to a multiple of 3.

1237
01:05:00.771 --> 01:05:01.539
Senior.

1238
01:05:01.819 --> 01:05:02.864
Question 10.

1239
01:05:03.144 --> 01:05:07.240
Walk me through a capacity estimate for checkout during a festival sale.

1240
01:05:07.520 --> 01:05:08.885
Thirty-second answer:

1241
01:05:09.165 --> 01:05:21.496
1.5 M orders that day, 20 % in the first hour, first minute at 5 times, then ~420 orders per second, ~850 checkout attempts per second.

1242
01:05:21.776 --> 01:05:23.845
That's ~6 pods for checkout.

1243
01:05:24.125 --> 01:05:35.347
The real risks are ~110 K req per second of catalog reads, hot inventory rows, DB connection budgets, and the 40-second rise time, so pre-scale.

1244
01:05:36.697 --> 01:05:37.827
Question 11.

1245
01:05:38.107 --> 01:05:39.451
We have autoscaling.

1246
01:05:39.731 --> 01:05:42.142
Why did the sale still fail in the first minutes?

1247
01:05:42.422 --> 01:05:43.787
Thirty-second answer:

1248
01:05:44.067 --> 01:05:56.931
Reactive scaling has latency: metrics scrape (~15 s), HPA sync (~15 s), node provisioning (1 to 4 min), JVM start and warm-up (20 to 90 s).

1249
01:05:57.211 --> 01:06:00.070
A spike rising in 40 s outruns all of it.

1250
01:06:00.350 --> 01:06:06.238
Meanwhile, overload, retries and latency-based liveness probes reduce capacity further.

1251
01:06:07.588 --> 01:06:08.676
Question 12.

1252
01:06:08.956 --> 01:06:13.201
How do you manage the database connection budget across 26 services?

1253
01:06:13.481 --> 01:06:14.847
Thirty-second answer:

1254
01:06:15.127 --> 01:06:17.708
Treat connections per database as a budget.

1255
01:06:17.988 --> 01:06:26.884
Size each service's pool from Little's Law, sum pods times pool per database including peak scale-out, and keep the total under a safe limit.

1256
01:06:27.164 --> 01:06:33.585
Use PgBouncer (transaction pooling) when the fleet is large, and enforce this in the capacity plan.

1257
01:06:34.935 --> 01:06:36.215
Question 13.

1258
01:06:36.495 --> 01:06:40.271
How would an estimate reveal a hot key problem before production?

1259
01:06:40.551 --> 01:06:41.917
Thirty-second answer:

1260
01:06:42.197 --> 01:06:44.735
Estimate per-key load, not just total load.

1261
01:06:45.015 --> 01:07:02.274
Multiply total rate times fraction touching the key times concentration (one store, one SKU), and compare with single-key capacity (row lock ~200 to 1,000 updates per second, Redis key on one shard ~100 K ops per second shared).

1262
01:07:03.624 --> 01:07:04.819
Question 14.

1263
01:07:05.099 --> 01:07:07.573
Your estimate was 3 times off in production.

1264
01:07:07.853 --> 01:07:10.179
What went wrong and how do you catch it earlier?

1265
01:07:10.459 --> 01:07:11.824
Thirty-second answer:

1266
01:07:12.104 --> 01:07:27.037
Usual causes: a wrong peak factor or window, per-pod capacity taken from a guess or stale load test, missing fan-out or retries, a new feature adding calls per page, or a marketing change nobody told engineering about.

1267
01:07:27.317 --> 01:07:36.875
Catch it with actual-versus-forecast dashboards, early canary traffic, load tests after major releases, and a forecast process that includes marketing.

1268
01:07:38.225 --> 01:07:39.121
Architect.

1269
01:07:39.401 --> 01:07:40.595
Question 15.

1270
01:07:40.875 --> 01:07:46.550
How would you set up capacity planning as an ongoing process for a 26-service platform?

1271
01:07:46.830 --> 01:07:48.195
Thirty-second answer:

1272
01:07:48.475 --> 01:07:53.510
Assign ownership (a capacity owner per tier-1 service and a platform owner overall).

1273
01:07:53.790 --> 01:07:58.227
Keep measured per-pod capacity profiles current through regular load tests.

1274
01:07:58.507 --> 01:08:01.579
Forecast quarterly and per event with business input.

1275
01:08:01.859 --> 01:08:04.526
Plan with policy (P90, zone headroom).

1276
01:08:04.806 --> 01:08:05.979
Check data tiers.

1277
01:08:06.259 --> 01:08:08.307
Ship plans as GitOps config.

1278
01:08:08.587 --> 01:08:11.489
Review actual versus forecast after each event.

1279
01:08:12.839 --> 01:08:14.033
Question 16.

1280
01:08:14.313 --> 01:08:18.111
How do you estimate the infrastructure cost of a new feature per order?

1281
01:08:18.391 --> 01:08:19.756
Thirty-second answer:

1282
01:08:20.036 --> 01:08:29.508
Estimate added requests per order (fan-out), CPU per request, storage and egress per order, and third-party costs (SMS, maps API).

1283
01:08:29.788 --> 01:08:33.500
Convert to ₹ with unit prices, then divide by orders.

1284
01:08:33.780 --> 01:08:37.001
Compare with the feature's revenue or retention impact.

1285
01:08:38.351 --> 01:08:39.695
Question 17.

1286
01:08:39.975 --> 01:08:42.642
The business expects 10 times growth in 2 years.

1287
01:08:42.922 --> 01:08:45.013
What breaks first and how do you plan?

1288
01:08:45.293 --> 01:08:46.658
Thirty-second answer:

1289
01:08:46.938 --> 01:08:49.093
Estimate each component at 10 times.

1290
01:08:49.373 --> 01:09:04.626
Usually the first to break are single-writer databases (write throughput, connections), hot keys, shared infra (Kafka cluster, Redis shard), cost lines like logs and CDN, and org or operational load, before stateless compute.

1291
01:09:04.906 --> 01:09:08.767
Plan changes in order of time to break times lead time to fix.

1292
01:09:10.117 --> 01:09:11.291
Question 18.

1293
01:09:11.571 --> 01:09:15.837
How do you present capacity estimates and their uncertainty to executives?

1294
01:09:16.117 --> 01:09:17.483
Thirty-second answer:

1295
01:09:17.763 --> 01:09:26.552
Give scenarios (P50 or P90 or stress) with their drivers, what each costs, and what happens if we're wrong (degradation, not outage).

1296
01:09:26.832 --> 01:09:28.539
Lead with the decision you need:

1297
01:09:28.819 --> 01:09:35.539
Approve 11 lakh rupees to protect ~7 crore rupees of GMV at risk in the first hour.

1298
01:09:36.889 --> 01:09:38.126
Question 19.

1299
01:09:38.406 --> 01:09:43.441
How do you estimate when there's no historical data (a new city launch or a new product)?

1300
01:09:43.721 --> 01:09:45.086
Thirty-second answer:

1301
01:09:45.366 --> 01:10:03.158
Use analogues (a similar city scaled by population or penetration), top-down market sizing checked against bottom-up (store capacity times riders times slots), wide ranges, and a staged launch (invite-only, one pincode cluster) that produces real data quickly.

1302
01:10:03.438 --> 01:10:08.494
Then design for elasticity plus hard limits (admission control, slot caps).

1303
01:10:09.844 --> 01:10:10.953
Question 20.

1304
01:10:11.233 --> 01:10:14.604
Is N+1 or zone-failure headroom worth the cost?

1305
01:10:14.884 --> 01:10:16.079
How do you decide?

1306
01:10:16.359 --> 01:10:17.724
Thirty-second answer:

1307
01:10:18.004 --> 01:10:19.092
Decide per tier.

1308
01:10:19.372 --> 01:10:30.529
Tier-1 paths (checkout, payment, catalog during events) get zones ÷ (zones minus 1) headroom because an AZ loss at peak is a total outage otherwise.

1309
01:10:30.809 --> 01:10:34.713
Tier-2 or 3 services accept degradation or shed load.

1310
01:10:34.993 --> 01:10:41.692
Weigh the headroom cost against the probability of an AZ event times revenue at risk times duration.

1311
01:10:43.042 --> 01:10:43.938
Part 9.

1312
01:10:44.218 --> 01:10:45.626
Summary and Practice.

1313
01:10:46.876 --> 01:10:47.964
Key takeaways.

1314
01:10:48.244 --> 01:10:52.404
Estimation finds which part of the design breaks, not a precise number.

1315
01:10:52.684 --> 01:10:53.516
Anchors:

1316
01:10:53.796 --> 01:11:07.023
1 M per day about 12 per second, a day about 10⁵ s, memory ~100 ns, same-AZ hop ~0.5 ms, new JVM or node = tens of seconds to minutes.

1317
01:11:07.303 --> 01:11:10.225
Peak factors come from traffic shape and rise time.

1318
01:11:10.505 --> 01:11:13.172
Scheduled sale starts are the dangerous case.

1319
01:11:13.452 --> 01:11:22.583
The read or write ratio decides the architecture: memory replica, cache or CDN, write-optimized store, or contention design.

1320
01:11:22.863 --> 01:11:28.772
Storage = records times size times retention times overhead (indexes, replicas).

1321
01:11:29.052 --> 01:11:31.164
Separate OLTP, blobs and archive.

1322
01:11:32.514 --> 01:11:35.501
Little's Law sizes pools, threads and partitions.

1323
01:11:35.781 --> 01:11:38.725
Size connection pools from it, never from defaults.

1324
01:11:39.005 --> 01:11:41.799
Target 50 to 60 % utilization.

1325
01:11:42.079 --> 01:11:44.703
Queueing makes tails explode near saturation.

1326
01:11:44.983 --> 01:11:50.615
Add zone-failure headroom for tier-1 services: zones ÷ (zones minus 1).

1327
01:11:50.895 --> 01:11:54.053
Reactive autoscaling can't absorb step changes.

1328
01:11:54.333 --> 01:11:57.063
Pre-scale, and keep load shedding as the backstop.

1329
01:11:57.343 --> 01:12:01.845
Present ranges with drivers and cost, and recalibrate after every event.

1330
01:12:03.195 --> 01:12:04.517
Decision cheat sheet.

1331
01:12:04.797 --> 01:12:06.163
Here is a quick table:

1332
01:12:06.443 --> 01:12:08.555
If the estimate shows..., and Then....

1333
01:12:08.835 --> 01:12:11.459
Dataset a few hundred MB, read-mostly:

1334
01:12:11.739 --> 01:12:13.552
In-memory replica per instance.

1335
01:12:13.832 --> 01:12:16.648
Read:write ≥ 100:1:

1336
01:12:16.928 --> 01:12:19.296
Cache + CDN + read replicas.

1337
01:12:19.576 --> 01:12:21.005
Write-heavy time series:

1338
01:12:21.285 --> 01:12:23.653
LSM or time-series store, batching.

1339
01:12:23.933 --> 01:12:25.939
Per-key rate near single-key limits:

1340
01:12:26.219 --> 01:12:31.616
Split keys, bucket, queue (Ch 46). Σ pools DB connection limit:

1341
01:12:31.896 --> 01:12:34.157
Little's Law sizing, PgBouncer.

1342
01:12:34.437 --> 01:12:36.357
Spike rise time scaling time:

1343
01:12:36.637 --> 01:12:38.771
Pre-scale (cron floor) + shedding.

1344
01:12:39.051 --> 01:12:42.272
Growth breaks a component before a migration could finish:

1345
01:12:42.552 --> 01:12:45.603
Start the migration now (runway versus lead time).

